## Nix flake — the polity's deployment carrier

The `flake.nix` at the repo root *is* how {{ polity.title or "the polity" }}'s swarm is provisioned. The polity is not a single host — it's a federation of agents distributed across heterogeneous *computer-systems* (this VPS, the captain's laptop, future cloud workers, browser-bound agents reaching in via MCP, and routable LLM endpoints). The flake is the substrate's projection of itself into Nix: each registered computer-system gets its own `devShells.<system>.<computer-system-slug>` output, so entering the shell on a host drops you into the polity's environment with `pyfars` + `python` on `PATH`{% if computer_systems %}:

| Computer-system | Role | Nix system | Entry |
|---|---|---|---|
{%- for cs in computer_systems %}
| [`{{ cs.slug }}`](operations/command/group/computer-systems/{{ cs.slug }}.md) | {{ cs.role }} | `{{ cs.nix_system }}` | `nix develop .#{{ cs.slug }}` |
{%- endfor %}

{% endif %}

The polity's substrate is one machine pinned to one set of inputs in `flake.lock`, then projected differently per host. Adding a host means adding one record under `operations/command/group/computer-systems/` with a `nix-system:` declaration and re-running `pyfars install-harness nix-flake`. The flake is regenerated; `nix develop .#<new-slug>` works on that host immediately.

### Installing Nix

The flake assumes a working multi-user Nix install. We use **[Determinate Nix](https://determinate.systems/)** as the canonical installer:

```bash
curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf -L https://install.determinate.systems/nix | sh -s -- install
```

Reasons: flakes enabled by default, robust uninstall, and a more agent-friendly maintenance surface than the Ubuntu/Debian-packaged Nix (which we found to be broken on first install at `vps-mini-cold-red-gate` — see the friction-log). After install, open a new shell so `PATH` picks up `/nix/var/nix/profiles/default/bin`, then:

```bash
nix develop .#<your-computer-system-slug>
```

If your host isn't yet registered as a polity computer-system, `nix develop` (no attribute) gives a generic devshell with the same toolchain.

### Why Nix carries the swarm, and not (yet) something else

The captain's preference is Guix; Nix is today's pragma. The flake-as-deployment-spec story is mature in Nix in a way Guix's manifest story isn't yet, and getting the polity's swarm-projection working *now* matters more than getting the long-term-correct packaging story right. A future Guix harness will likely wrap the Nix layer rather than replace it.

